  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a complex root $x=-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}$ is a root, we know that its complex conjugate $x=-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}$ is also a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}))(x-(-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i})-x(-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i})+(-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i})(-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-\\cancel{x\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}}+2x+\\cancel{x\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}}+4-7{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4+7 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
